{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB14568 - MONDO_18437",
  "verbalization": "Ivosidenib, a selective inhibitor of the histone deacetylase HDAC1 and HDAC2, has been associated with the treatment of inherited acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and acute myeloblastic leukemia without maturation. The evidence suggests that Ivosidenib may exert its therapeutic effect through a mechanism involving the inhibition of histone deacetylases, which are often dysregulated in AML. This inhibition could lead to the reactivation of tumor suppressor genes and the disruption of the malignant transformation of leukemic cells. The observed relationships between Ivosidenib and AML with Npm1 somatic mutations highlight the potential for repurposing Ivosidenib in this context, particularly given the role of Npm1 mutations in driving leukemogenesis. The evidence is supported by the shared indication of AML with Npm1 mutations in multiple paths, suggesting a biologically plausible link between Ivosidenib and the disease. The mechanism of action is consistent across different subtypes of AML, reinforcing the potential for Ivosidenib to be a viable treatment option for patients with this condition.",
